A||||19||gtcash||V8 Sanderson headers?||||6||
Z||000000||gtcash||12-04-2001||01:10 AM||||CC90 is the number...do you have them? Do you have duals with 2 mufflers, or do you exhaust 2 into one? Mine was 2 into 1, I want to have duals...anyone done it?||||reg||6
Z||000001||fix||12-04-2001||01:15 AM||||I'm planning on using CC90's and I was going to run duals.  Archie always runs the duals and it looks to be fairly straight forward.  Can't really tell you much else tho.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtcash||12-04-2001||01:31 AM||||Thanks for reply, does Archie sell the complete exhaust without the headers?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hartz||12-04-2001||08:07 AM||||Hi,<P>I have the Sanderson headers, and actuallly have a Y-pipe prior to the muffler - for single pipe.  When I redo the exhaust this winter, I will probably just get a f-body muffler with a 1 in, 2 out configuration.<P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||1
Z||000004||fix||12-04-2001||09:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gtcash:<BR><B>Thanks for reply, does Archie sell the complete exhaust without the headers?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't think he does normally, but you should ask him about that.  If you've ever seen his V8 installation video, then you'd see that it's a very simple design and you would probably be able to do yourself if you have access to a welder.  I think archie sells the entire exhaust including ram horn manifolds for $550 and after seeing the design I think I could do much better on my own for less cash.  No offense archie.||||reg||1
Z||000005||voyagerspe||12-04-2001||01:48 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MVC-005S_4.JPG">  <P>ive got them. here is a pic with just the headers.<P>Ive got them installed with cats and 12 inch.  bulletstyle mufflers.  Ive got the bullets mounted where the tips would be.  Ive got supertrap tips welded right onto the end of the bullets to give them enough back pressure to work.  <P>as you can see with the pic with an auto its a tight fit (but doable) when using the headers<BR><p>[This message has been edited by voyagerspe (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||JasonS||12-04-2001||02:42 PM||||I've got the Sanderson cc90's also on my v8. I did my own exhaust for now.  Couple small bends and straight cherry bombs.  It's not pretty, but it sounds good.<P>Jason||||reg||14
Z||000007||California Kid||12-04-2001||03:11 PM||||Just curious why you want to have dual mufflers?? If you run from the Sanderson's to a 3 inch collector into a modified Flowmaster (1 in 2 out, use camaro/firebird flowmaster) to stock tips, everything works real nice. You aren't going to adding any performance by adding another muffler.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||12-04-2001||03:36 PM||||I would want to keep the factory exhaust routing - or something similar.  That would seem to be easier to run both a catatonic converter and muffler.  I have to be emissions legal in my area.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||jelly2m8||12-04-2001||08:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hartz:<BR><B>Hi,<P>I have the Sanderson headers, and actuallly have a Y-pipe prior to the muffler - for single pipe.  When I redo the exhaust this winter, I will probably just get a f-body muffler with a 1 in, 2 out configuration.<P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Any pics Hartz? I'm thinking of loosing my duals for a 2 into 1, sounds like you are running what I have planned.<P> Hopefully by doing this I can run a longer exhaust system, build some more back pressure, and quieten the beast down some more.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Hartz||12-04-2001||08:24 PM||||Jelly,<P>Unfortunately, I don't have any underside shots.  You can kind of see how the exhaust is routed in this picture, but unfortunately the cradle is in the way a bit.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Engine_4.jpg"> <P>It's actually pretty quiet, considering.  No one would ever mistake it for the original engine or anything, but it's not too bad.<P>Hartz<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||jelly2m8||12-04-2001||08:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hartz:<BR><B>Jelly,<P>Unfortunately, I don't have any underside shots.  You can kind of see how the exhaust is routed in this picture, but unfortunately the cradle is in the way a bit.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Engine_4.jpg">  <P>It's actually pretty quiet, considering.  No one would ever mistake it for the original engine or anything, but it's not too bad.<P>Hartz<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Looks good Hartz, looks like the pipes connect just before the 90* bend into the muffler.<P> Thanks abunch!<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Bazooka||12-04-2001||11:13 PM||||Voyager and Hartz,   Nice looking work. Would you have any pics of the piping below the collector with an Automatic? Is there any cutting of the rear trunk that's needed? ||||reg||1
Z||000013||California Kid||12-04-2001||11:37 PM||||Hartz.....Just noticed the thermal tape on your headers. Don't know if you heard the cautions on using thermal tape, but it can shorten the life of your headers. Your choice, as I don't know reasons you chose it.<P>I'm running bare factory black Sanderson's with no thermal issues. The car has been running this way since '93.  I'll probably get the entire system (except trumpets)"Jet Hot" coated in color for next season, but the reason is not thermal, just show.<P>Just thought I'd throw this in.||||reg||1
Z||000014||gtcash||12-04-2001||11:58 PM||||The cradle only has one hump for one pipe on the right side...The 2 exhaust pipes have joined as one and go under the hump and make a left hand turn thru the muffler and out the left side. I've always believed 2 seperate exhausts would be better breathing than 2 into 1. I'm 60 years old, so I've had this belief for a long time. Tell me I've been right all these years, please?||||reg||6
Z||000015||California Kid||12-05-2001||12:34 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gtcash:<BR><B>The cradle only has one hump for one pipe on the right side...The 2 exhaust pipes have joined as one and go under the hump and make a left hand turn thru the muffler and out the left side. I've always believed 2 seperate exhausts would be better breathing than 2 into 1. I'm 60 years old, so I've had this belief for a long time. Tell me I've been right all these years, please?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>You are correct, but if you go from the headers to a 3 in diameter collector pipe, into a modified very low restriction muffler, the result is the same. Of course the pipes coming off the headers must be close to about the same lenght (for maximum effect), which presents a challenge on the Fiero. I don't have any pictures of mine, it's done pretty sweet. Listen to this sound file, think you'll agree it's breathing just fine. <A HREF="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/cal-engine.wav" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/cal-engine.wav</A> <BR>Takes a little while to load, but you can minimize the load window and keep surfing.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Hartz||12-05-2001||09:12 AM||||Cali-Kid - I hear you on the wrapped headers.  As a matter of fact, I am a big believer in NOT wrapping pipes.  <P>I've got a set of ceramic coated Hooker block huggers sitting in my work room just waiting for a spare afternoon.  Know of anywhere in the Detroit area that might be able to finish it off with a nice stainless system?<P>Hartz||||reg||1
